.TH INSTALL 8
.SH NAME
install \- put SIEOS on a PC's NVMe drive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B install list
.br
.B install
.RB [ \-n ]
.B free
.I drive n
.br
.B install
.RB [ \-n ]
.B whole
.I drive
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B install
(x86-64, root only) puts SIEOS, started from the USB key, on the PC's own
NVMe drive, beside the systems already there. The desktop's Install
window runs it and shows what it prints; it also works on a terminal.
.TP
.B list
The drives: model, size, partitions and free extents.
.TP
.B free
Into free extent
.I n
of
.I drive
(at least 4 GiB): one entry added to the drive's GPT, nothing else moved
or resized. The drive must have an EFI System Partition (FAT32).
.TP
.B whole
Erase
.IR drive :
a new GPT, an EFI partition (FAT32, 300 MiB) and SIEOS's partition (the
rest).
.PP
Before writing it prints the plan (exactly what changes) and asks:
.B yes
for free space; for a whole drive, its model name typed exactly. Then:
the new SieFS partition, with a fresh system (the key's snapshot
.BR factory :
no accounts, the first start makes them), checked block by block;
.B \eEFI\eSIEOS\e
in the EFI partition (the loader, the kernel, the modules,
.B BOOT.CFG
with
.BI root= GUID\fR);
the GPT last (backup, then primary), read back and compared. Start the
PC from the USB key once more: its loader adds "SIEOS" to the firmware's
boot menu.
.PP
Refused: the drive SIEOS runs from, a GPT not valid in every way, an
MBR-only or hybrid-MBR drive (for free space), a GPT with no free entry,
free space under 4 GiB. Every write is checked against the ranges the
plan declared.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Print the plan only: nothing is written.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when installed; 1 on an error; 2 for a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /.snapshots/factory
the system copied.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
install list
install \-n free 0 2
install whole 1
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boot.cfg (5),
.BR siefs (5),
.BR boot (7)
